# Fonti generator rules

## Code point handling. Get this exactly right.

Most of these styles live above U+FFFF, which means each character is two
UTF-16 code units in JavaScript. Naive code breaks in two ways and both
must be handled explicitly.

- Never use charCodeAt, and never index a string with square brackets when
  mapping. Iterate with for (const ch of str), or spread the string, or use
  codePointAt, so a styled character is never split in half.
- Never use str.length as the user-facing character count. It reports two
  per styled letter. Report both numbers with plain-language labels, for
  example "12 characters, 24 units". The first is the code point count, the
  second is the UTF-16 length. This is a genuine competitor gap. Make it
  visible, not hidden in a tooltip.
- Platform names are banned in any counter label, badge or note. Which
  platforms count Plane 1 characters as two is an open, unverified question
  on this project. Naming a platform is a compatibility claim, and
  unverified compatibility claims are forbidden.

Build the mapping as an offset calculation, not a hand-typed lookup table.
Each block in Mathematical Alphanumeric Symbols holds uppercase A to Z at
a base, then lowercase a to z twenty-six code points later.

## Style record shape

Each entry in styles.ts carries an id, a display name, a category, an
uppercase base, a lowercase base, a substitutions map of code point
overrides, a digits field where null means digits pass through unchanged,
a risk field kept as an editorial note for the article writer, a public
`caveat` string for the badge (or null), and an optional `caseNote` when
one case folds onto the other.

## Badge rule (public caveat)

A badge may state only a property recorded in Unicode's own character data —
for example an alphabet drawn from more than one block, or emoji presentation
attached to a specific character. A badge may never predict what a device,
platform or application will do. The distinction is not whether the wording is
reassuring or cautious, because a warning is an assertion too; it is whether
anyone can verify it without owning the device.

Two badges satisfy that rule today: the multi-block Classic Script alphabet
("Uneven") and Circled capital M ("Capital M may show as emoji"). Everything
else renders nothing. Silence is the honest default.

Do not revive "Safe", "Check old devices", platform names, or any other
prediction about rendering on hardware. Plane membership alone is not a badge:
ten of thirteen styles already sit on the supplementary plane, so warning on
one card would imply the rest were verified fine, which nobody has established.

The blueprint's risk column remains an editorial note for the article writer.
Promoting a planning value into a public label turns it into a promise. The
UI reads `caveat` only; `risk` is never shown as a badge.

## The six cursive styles, with exact bases

- bold-script, Bold Script, uppercase U+1D4D0, lowercase U+1D4EA
- script, Classic Script, uppercase U+1D49C, lowercase U+1D4B6
- bold-italic, Bold Italic, uppercase U+1D468, lowercase U+1D482
- italic, Italic Script, uppercase U+1D434, lowercase U+1D44E
- sans-italic, Handwriting, uppercase U+1D608, lowercase U+1D622
- sans-bold-italic, Slanted, uppercase U+1D63C, lowercase U+1D656

## The substitution gaps that every cheap generator gets wrong

Unicode did not duplicate letters it had already encoded elsewhere, so
some slots inside these blocks are reserved and empty. Calculating the
offset without overriding them makes those letters render as blanks or
tofu.

Classic Script needs eleven overrides, all from Letterlike Symbols:
B is U+212C, E is U+2130, F is U+2131, H is U+210B, I is U+2110,
L is U+2112, M is U+2133, R is U+211B, lowercase e is U+212F,
lowercase g is U+210A, lowercase o is U+2134.

Italic Script needs one override: lowercase h is U+210E.

After implementing this, print all fifty-two characters of every style to
the console so they can be checked against these lists. Do not say it
works. Show the output.

## Adding a new style

- A new style may be added to `styles.ts` only with its Unicode block name
  and its base code points (uppercase, lowercase, digit, as applicable)
  recorded in a comment directly above the record. Do not add a style from
  memory alone; source every base code point from an actual Unicode chart
  and say so. If a code point cannot be sourced with certainty, leave the
  style out rather than guess.
- Any character missing from that block (a reserved or unassigned slot)
  must be declared, never left to pass through silently: either give it a
  real substitute code point in `substitutions`, or leave it plain and
  record exactly what happens in the style's public `caveat` (or `caseNote`
  for a whole-case fold). `scripts/verify-generator.ts` enforces this for
  every style automatically.

## Decorations are not styles

- A decoration is a wrapper: a prefix and a suffix of real Unicode characters
  placed around the visitor's text, which is copied through exactly as typed
  with its letters unconverted. Decorations live in `src/data/decorations.ts`.
  They never enter `styles.ts`, and they are never a category membership on
  an alphabet record. The visible catalogue counter must equal
  `styles.length + decorations.length + effects.length`, never a hardcoded
  number and never `styles.length` alone. That counter drifted when
  decorations and effects were added while the interface still rendered
  `styles.length`. Never present a decoration as an alphabet or a font.
- Every decoration character is recorded with its Unicode block name and its
  code point in a comment directly above the record, exactly as a style's base
  code points are. Source each one from an actual Unicode chart; if a code
  point cannot be sourced with certainty, leave the decoration out.
- A character with emoji presentation is declared, never hidden: it is named in
  the report and in that decoration's public `caveat`, so the card carries the
  same quiet badge a style would. `scripts/verify-generator.ts` prints every
  prefix and suffix character with its code point and fails on U+FFFD, and on
  any character with emoji presentation that has no caveat.

## Digits and unconvertible input

Cursive digits do not exist in Unicode. There is no cursive five. For
every cursive style, digits pass through unchanged, and the tool shows a
small persistent note on those cards saying numbers stay plain, so nobody
thinks the tool is broken. Other styles such as circled and bold do have
digits, which is why digits is a field and not a hardcoded assumption.

Anything the generator cannot convert, including accented letters and any
non-Latin script, passes through unchanged. Never substitute a lookalike
character as a guess.

## Performance rules for the tool

An earlier prototype re-rendered one hundred and thirty style cards on
every keystroke and janked badly on mobile. Do not repeat that.

- Debounce the input at roughly 120 ms before re-rendering.
- Use an IntersectionObserver so only cards currently in the viewport get
  recalculated. Offscreen cards recalculate when they scroll into view.
- Build the card list once at page load. On updates, set textContent on
  the existing output nodes. Never rebuild the DOM, never use innerHTML in
  a loop, never recreate card elements.
- Render the card list server-side in Astro so the page is useful before
  any JavaScript runs. JavaScript only wires up interactivity.
- No layout shift. Cards must have their final dimensions before JS runs.
- Never read a layout property after writing to the DOM in the same frame.
  All layout reads happen before all DOM writes. Card visibility comes from
  a Set updated in the IntersectionObserver callback from
  `entry.isIntersecting` only — never from `getBoundingClientRect` or any
  other measured geometry. The layout-reading APIs
  `getBoundingClientRect`, `getClientRects`, `offsetWidth`, `offsetHeight`,
  `offsetTop`, `offsetLeft`, `offsetParent`, `clientWidth`, `clientHeight`,
  `scrollWidth`, `scrollHeight`, `scrollTop`, `scrollLeft`, `scrollIntoView`,
  `getComputedStyle`, `checkVisibility`, `focus`, and `innerText` are banned
  inside the input handler, the debounce callback, the card render/update
  path, and the filter path. They are permitted only in page-load setup or
  inside an explicit `requestAnimationFrame` that carries a one-line comment
  naming the read and why it cannot be avoided. Do not wrap a whole update
  in `requestAnimationFrame` to paper over a read-after-write.
- Filtering is visibility only. It toggles `hidden` on existing cards and
  sections. It is never a second render path, never innerHTML, and never
  recreates nodes. Search and the active chip combine with AND.
- Every interactive control needs a state that is both visible on screen
  and exposed to assistive technology (for example `aria-pressed` on the
  favourite star, not a class alone).
- A control that exists in the markup is not shipped until its behaviour is
  proven by the code path that reads it. Listing the markup is not evidence
  that the control is wired.

## Tool features, in build order

Build these in this order. Do not start a later one before an earlier one
is correct and reviewed.

1. One input field above the fold with a real label. Prefill it with the
   page's own test phrase rather than leaving it empty, for example
   "Soda Pop 40" on the bubble page and "Wumpus" on the Discord page.
2. Category sections driven by toolCategories in the page frontmatter.
   Each category gets a visible heading with its real name. No generic
   labels such as "Style 47" anywhere.
3. A card per style showing the real style name, the user's text rendered
   in it, and a copy button.
4. Copy via the async Clipboard API with a document.execCommand fallback.
   Announce success in the aria-live region.
5. A dual character counter on every style card. Each card counts its own
   styled output, not the raw input and not another style's output. Classic
   Script reports fewer UTF-16 units than the other cursive styles for the
   same letters, because eleven of its characters come from Letterlike
   Symbols in the Basic Multilingual Plane. A single page-level counter
   would therefore print a wrong number next to most cards.
6. A quiet per-card risk indicator driven by the risk field. Render it only
   when a style actually carries a value. No value means no badge, never a
   default badge.
7. Case transform buttons: UPPER, lower, Title, aLt. Pure string
   operations on the input before styling.
8. A search box that filters the visible style cards by name and category.
   Provide a real empty state. Filtering must not cause layout shift.
9. Favourites, toggled by a star on each card, and a recently-used list.
   Both persist in localStorage. Render both section shells server-side
   with their empty states, so reading localStorage on load causes no
   layout shift.
10. Platform previews: Instagram bio, Discord nickname, X display name and
    TikTok display name, showing the user's text in a chosen style inside
    a realistic frame. No follower counts, no like counts, no timestamps,
    no invented engagement numbers of any kind. Frame chrome only.
11. A decorated section, clearly labelled and separate from the plain
    styles, where a style is a base alphabet wrapped in symbols. Never
    present a wrapped variant as if it were a distinct alphabet.

The sticky copy bar is deferred on scope, not dropped, and not because of
layout shift. A sticky bar whose space is reserved in normal flow from the
start does not cause layout shift. It is deferred because it is not one of
the eleven steps. It stays on the deferred list.

## Deferred until every character is verified correct

Do not build these until the generator has been checked against the code
point lists above and the owner has confirmed it.

- The combo builder: base style, plus an optional separator between
  letters, plus an optional wrapper around the text, with a live preview
  and a copy button. When it is built, the dual character counter must be
  visible inside the combo panel, because separators multiply the
  character count and silently break platform bio limits. Label combo
  output as decoration, not as a font.
- A random style button.
- Keyboard shortcuts. If they are ever added, document only the ones
  actually tested.
- The sticky copy bar (deferred on scope: it is not one of the eleven
  steps above, not because of layout shift).

The reason for deferring is ordering, not dislike. The earlier prototype
shipped a combo builder and a random button while its characters were
still wrong.
